  "reasoning": "The patient presents with three key symptoms: trouble breathing during exercise, excessive sweating/heat intolerance, and recurrent yeast infections. While trouble breathing on exertion might initially suggest bronchial asthma, asthma does not account for the recurrent yeast infections or the pronounced sweating/heat sensitivity. Similarly, while yeast infections are fungal in nature, diagnosing a simple 'fungal infection' ignores the systemic pattern; recurrent yeast infections are a classic clinical indicator of an underlying metabolic disorder. Diabetes mellitus perfectly explains this combination: elevated blood glucose levels create an environment that promotes frequent yeast overgrowth, autonomic neuropathy and blood sugar fluctuations commonly cause excessive sweating and heat intolerance, and poor circulation or deconditioning associated with uncontrolled diabetes can lead to breathlessness during physical activity. Therefore, diabetes is the single unit that comprehensively establishes all the presented facts.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]diabetes[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
